prompt:
You are a financial analyst revising a forecast based on a peer critique.

Original forecast: {
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 3200,
      "net_income_cr": 172,
      "eps": 116.9
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 3750,
      "net_income_cr": 206,
      "eps": 140.0
    },
    {
      "year": 2025,
      "revenue_cr": 4300,
      "net_income_cr": 240,
      "eps": 163.1
    }
  ],
  "top_assumptions": [
    "Revenue growth of ~17-18% per year driven by strong order book (remaining performance obligations of Rs 8,751 Cr as at March 2022, representing ~3.2x FY2022 revenues), government infrastructure/power capex tailwinds, and diversification into railways, T&D and mining segments \u2014 revenue recovery from COVID-depressed FY2021 is largely complete in FY2022 and a more normalized 17-18% CAGR is used going forward rather than the optically high 50% YoY jump in FY2022.",
    "Net profit margin stabilizes and modestly improves from 5.1% (consolidated) in FY2022 toward 5.5-5.6% by FY2025, as operating leverage kicks in on higher revenues, finance costs remain broadly flat (debt-equity already declining to 0.51x), and there are no repeat COVID-related losses \u2014 EPS computed on 1,47,10,764 weighted average shares."
  ],
  "reasoning": "FY2022 consolidated Revenue from Operations was Rs 2,710 Cr vs Rs 1,884 Cr in FY2021, a 43.8% YoY jump; FY2021 was severely COVID-impacted (net loss of Rs 49 Cr consolidated), making this rebound anomalous and not a reliable forward projection base. Only two years of P&L data are explicitly available in the report (FY2022 and FY2021), so a multi-year CAGR cannot be precisely computed; however, the 10-year highlights table shows standalone turnover of Rs 2,631 Cr in FY2022 vs Rs 1,754 Cr in FY2021, and prior normal years (pre-COVID) were growing at roughly 10-18% annually. Given the strong unexecuted order book of Rs 8,751 Cr (standalone, ~3.3x revenues), management's explicit diversification thrust into railways, T&D and mining, and India's large infrastructure capex pipeline, a forward growth rate of ~17-18% is applied \u2014 above pre-COVID trend to reflect diversification and order-book visibility, but well below the anomalous FY2022 rebound rate. Net profit margin is projected to improve gradually from 5.1% toward 5.5% as operating leverage materializes and finance costs stay contained given improving debt-equity (0.51x in FY2022 vs 0.60x in FY2021)."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "POWERMECH",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Revenue growth of 17-18% per year is sustainable, anchored by an Rs 8,751 Cr order book representing ~3.2x FY2022 revenues",
      "failure_scenario": "Order-to-revenue conversion slows materially due to project execution bottlenecks (labour shortages, raw material inflation, client-side delays in land acquisition or approvals), or a significant portion of the order book is cancelled/deferred as government capex priorities shift post-election cycle, compressing actual revenue growth to 8-10% and making the 3-year revenue targets unachievable",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net profit margin improves steadily from 5.1% in FY2022 toward 5.5-5.6% by FY2025 driven by operating leverage, with finance costs remaining broadly flat",
      "failure_scenario": "Steel, cement and subcontractor costs spike due to commodity supercycles or supply chain disruptions, while fixed-price EPC contracts prevent cost pass-through; simultaneously, competitive bidding pressure in railways and T&D segments forces lower bid margins, keeping net margins compressed at or below FY2022 levels rather than expanding \u2014 a 50-100 bps margin shortfall would reduce projected net income by Rs 20-45 Cr annually",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "FY2022 is treated as a normalized base year and the 43.8% YoY revenue rebound from COVID-depressed FY2021 is considered complete, making 17-18% forward growth a conservative and realistic starting point",
      "failure_scenario": "FY2022 itself overstates normalized earning power because it benefited from a temporary surge in deferred project completions and one-time catch-up billing; if underlying run-rate revenue was closer to Rs 2,300-2,400 Cr on a truly normalized basis, the forecast starting point is inflated by 10-15%, causing all three forward-year projections to be systematically overstated from the outset",
      "risk": "Medium"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 42,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast rests on an optimistic but not implausible growth narrative, yet the simultaneous assumptions of sustained 17-18% top-line growth AND margin expansion represent a double-compounding of positive outcomes that rarely materialize together in capital-intensive, competitively-bid EPC businesses. The reliance on only two years of P&L data (one of which is COVID-distorted) as the analytical foundation, combined with the absence of any scenario analysis or downside case, materially undermines confidence in the point estimates provided."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
